# Regression discontinuity: what happens at your 65th birthday
subtitle: "How one birthday works like an experiment: the jump in coverage at 65, the one assumption it rests on, and what it showed about deaths of emergency patients."
tags: ["regression discontinuity","medicare","health insurance","mortality","causal inference"]
teaches: ["methods/regression-discontinuity","concepts/continuity-assumption","concepts/local-effects"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"Stress-testing a discontinuity","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/stress-testing-a-discontinuity.cast"}

## One birthday, two insurance systems
voice: female
domain:
    x:
