    card title "One birthday, two insurance systems"

In America, your sixty-fifth birthday changes almost nothing about you, except your health insurance: Medicare starts, for almost everyone.
    draw ax t60 t70 cut cut_lab parallel true

So can that one day tell us whether health insurance keeps people alive?
    highlight cut

The obvious test compares people old enough for Medicare, say at seventy, with people too young for it, say at sixty.
    draw b60 l60 b70 l70 parallel true

But a seventy-year-old is ten years older, and age alone raises the risk of dying.
    highlight b70 l70

    quiz "Which comparison would isolate what Medicare does?"
        intro: Before we go on, your pick.
        right: A month either side of sixty-five: the two groups are the same age in every way that matters, and only one of them has Medicare.
        wrong: For each pair, ask what else differs between the two groups besides Medicare.
        * Age 60 against age 70
        + A month short of 65 against a month past it
        * Insured against uninsured 64-year-olds

So shrink the age gap until ageing can't matter: a month (@draw bl br parallel true@) short of sixty-five against a month past it.
    erase b60 l60 b70 l70

    erase bl br

First, though, the birthday has to change something. Up the side, the share of Americans with any health insurance.
    draw yl y85 y90 y95 cov_l

At sixty-three and sixty-four, about eighty-eight in a hundred are covered.
    draw r_left

Then the line breaks. Just past sixty-five, coverage jumps by (@draw jump r_jump@) about nine and a half percentage points.
    draw cov_r

Card, Dobkin and Maestas measured that jump in national health surveys from 1999 to 2003, with a standar (@highlight r_se@)d error of just over half a point.
    text r_se "(SE 0.6)" #8a5fa8 at.data [67.6,91.3] cites ["cdm2008"] font_size 34
    text r_src "Card et al. 2008" #8f887c at.data [67.6,89.2] cites ["cdm2008"] font_size 30

Income, marriage and work, meanwhile, pass through sixty-five with barely a ripple.
    text nj_h "No jump:" #3d3833 x 865 y 470 font_size 40
    text nj_1 "income" #3d3833 cites ["cdm2008"] x 865 y 410 font_size 38
    text nj_2 "marriage" #3d3833 cites ["cdm2008"] x 865 y 360 font_size 38
    text nj_3 "work" #3d3833 cites ["cdm2008"] x 865 y 310 font_size 38

Nothing about a person changes overnight at sixty-five. A legal rule does, and nobody can choose which side of their own birthday they are on.
    highlight cut

    erase nj_h nj_1 nj_2 nj_3

A design built on a cutoff like that is called a regression d (@highlight cut@)iscontinuity, and age, the number the rule read (@highlight rv t60 t70@)s, is its running variable.
    math rv #3d3833 tex a_i size 44 colors.a_i #3d3833 x 865 y 190
    text rv_t "running variable" #3d3833 x 865 y 140 font_size 30

If coverage jumps at the line and nothing else does, then a jump in health at the line points to Medicare.
    highlight jump r_jump

That 'nothing else' is the whole design, so it needs stating exactly.
    fade rv rv_t r_src r_se to 0.3

## The one assumption: continuity
