    card title "The one assumption: continuity"

To see what has to be true at that l (@highlight cut@)ine, take one kind of patient, with round numbers of our own, close to the real ones.
    draw ax y3 y4 y5 y6 t60 t70 cut cut_lab parallel true

They arrive at an emergency room with a heart attack or a stroke, and w (@highlight y3 y4 y5 y6@)e count who dies within a week.
    draw yl

The red line is each age's death rate without Medicare, Y-zero: five in a hundred at sixty-five, creeping up with age.
    draw y0_l y0_rs lab0

The blue line is the same patients with Medicare, Y-one. Say it sits one point lower at every age.
    draw y1_ls y1_r lab1

But each age shows us only o (@draw y0_r y1_l parallel true@)ne of them: the red line before the birthday, the blue line after.
    hide y0_rs y1_ls

So what we actually see is red up to sixty-five, then blue: five in a h (@draw step@)undred, then four.
    draw r5 r4

    hide t60 t70

That step down of one point is the g (@highlight step@)ap between the two lines at exactly sixty-five: the effect of b (@highlight tau@)ecoming eligible for Medicare, at that age.
    draw tau

Everyone crosses into eligibility on the same birthday, which makes this a sharp design.
    highlight cut

And it is that gap only if the red line, the one we stop seeing, would have carried straight on through the birthday.
    highlight y0_r

That is the assumption the whole design rests on, called continuity: without Medicare, the average death rate wo (@draw cont1@)uld not jump at sixty-five,  (@highlight y0_l y0_r@)and the same holds for the death rate with it.
    morph tau tex "\\lim_{a \\uparrow 65} E[{Y_i(0)} \\mid a] = \\lim_{a \\downarrow 65} E[{Y_i(0)} \\mid a]"

    erase tau cont1

    show t60 t70

Now suppose something else also happened at sixty-five, say many people retired that day and retiring changed their health (@fade r4 r5 to 0.15@). That would lift both lines past the bir (@point at.ref grab gesture circle@)thday, so the blue line we see rises too.
    draw h_step v_step h_true v_true h_k v_k grab hk

Grab the dashed red line just past sixty-five and lift it, and watch the step we would see.
    explore params ["k"] watch "Watch the red line lift past sixty-five: the step seen shrinks, while Medicare's effect stays at minus one."

Lift it six tenths of a point, and the step we see shrinks to minus zer (@highlight v_step v_k@)o point four, though Medicare's own effect never moved from minus one.
    animate k 0.6 duration 3

So anything else that changes at sixty-five lands inside the est (@fade r4 r5 to 1@)imate. That is why the smooth income, marriage and work matter.
    animate k 0 duration 2

    quiz "Suppose a state also gave everyone a free yearly check-up from their 65th birthday on. What would the step at 65 measure?" intro "A new case to try."
        right: Both together: the check-ups start at the same line, so the red line would jump on its own, and continuity fails for Medicare alone.
        wrong: Continuity asks that nothing but Medicare changes at the line. What else changes here?
        * Medicare alone
        * The check-ups alone
        + Medicare and the check-ups together

    erase h_step v_step h_true v_true h_k v_k grab hk

Still, this asks less than comparing how two groups change over time, which needs them to move in parallel for years: here, nothing may break at one single point.
    highlight y0_l y0_r

The price is reach. The step tells us about peopl (@highlight step@)e at sixty-five, not at fifty, and not at eighty.
    text only "Only at 65" #8a5fa8 at.data [67.8,3.05] font_size 36

And real data never hand us these clean lines. They hand us noisy points, and the step has to be read from them.
    highlight y0_l y1_r

## What the birthday did in California
