sources:
    - id: cdm2009
      title: Does Medicare Save Lives?
      authors: David Card, Carlos Dobkin, Nicole Maestas, Quarterly Journal of Economics 124(2)
      year: 2009
      finding: '407,386 unplanned emergency admissions for non-deferrable conditions, California 1992-2002: 7-day mortality falls by 1.1 percentage points (SE 0.2) at 65 (1.0, SE 0.2, with controls) on a base of 5.1%, about a 20% reduction; a gap of 0.8-1.0 points persists for about 9 months. Arrivals are smooth at 65; Medicare as primary payer rises 44 points (SE 0.4), uninsured falls 7.4 points (SE 0.2); procedures rise slightly and list charges about 3%.'
    - id: cdm2008
      title: 'The Impact of Nearly Universal Insurance Coverage on Health Care Utilization: Evidence from Medicare'
      authors: David Card, Carlos Dobkin, Nicole Maestas, American Economic Review 98(5)
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Hospital discharge records from California, Florida and New York, 1992-2002: all hospital admissions rise 7.6% (SE 0.3) at 65, most for elective procedures such as bypass surgery and joint replacement.'
      doi: 10.1257/aer.98.5.2242

