    card title "What the birthday did in California"

Here is what real data look like: points, one for each narrow band of ages.
    draw ax y4 y5 y6 t60 t70 cut cut_lab yl parallel true

Card, Dobkin and Maestas gathered about four hundred thousand emergen (@draw nn@)cy admissions in California hospitals, from 1992 to 2002.
    draw dl parallel true

Each point is the share of patients of one (@point at.ref p13 gesture circle@) age who died within a week. These points come from our round numbers; theirs look much the same.
    draw dr ill parallel true

To read the step, fit a line to each side separately, using only ages near  (@draw fit_r@)the birthday. They used sixty to seventy, with a gentle curve on each side.
    draw fit_l

Then read the gap where the two fitte (@highlight step@)d lines meet the cutoff.
    draw step

Their own estimate, from the real data: one point one percentage points fewer deaths within a week, with  (@highlight e_se@)a standard error of zero point two.
    draw e_h e_v e_se

Just under sixty-five, about five in a hundred of these patients died within a week, so that (@draw e_frac@) is roughly one death in five prevented.
    draw e_base

And the gap had not closed nine months later.
    draw e_last

    erase e_base e_frac e_last

But the choice that mattered most in this study was not the line fitting. It was which patients to count.
    highlight dl dr

At sixty-five there are about seven and a half percent (@highlight a_v@) more hospital admissions overall, with a standard error of zero point three, in records from California, Florida and New York.
    draw a_h a_v a_se a_src

    quiz "Suppose Medicare also brought milder cases into hospital after 65. Which way would that push the measured step in deaths?"
        intro: Think about who walks in.
        right: Toward fewer deaths: milder patients die less often, so adding them after sixty-five would make Medicare look better than it is.
        wrong: Milder cases die less often. On which side of the line would they be added?
        + Toward fewer deaths after 65
        * Toward more deaths after 65
        * Neither way

    erase a_h a_v a_se a_src

So they kept only conditions that cannot wait: ones admitte (@highlight sun effect underline@)d as often on a Sunday as on a Monday, such as heart attacks and strokes.
    draw sun

    erase sun

For those, the number of patients arriving runs smoothly through sixty-five, (@highlight dl dr@) so both sides hold the same mix of the very sick.
    draw arr

    erase arr

And their insurance did change: Medicare as the main payer rose by about forty-four points, (@draw tb_2@) and the uninsured share fell by about seven points, from about one in ten.
    draw tb_h tb_1

With it came a few more procedures, and hospital  (@highlight step e_v@)bills about three percent higher.
    text tb_3 "Bills  +3 %" #3d3833 cites ["cdm2009"] x 870 y 160 font_size 34

## Who was saved, and how far it reaches
voice: female
sources:
    - id: cdm2009
      title: Does Medicare Save Lives?
      authors: David Card, Carlos Dobkin, Nicole Maestas, Quarterly Journal of Economics 124(2)
      year: 2009
      finding: Among non-deferrable emergency admissions in California, 1992-2002, 7-day mortality falls 1.1 percentage points (SE 0.2) at 65. The uninsured share falls 7.4 points (SE 0.2); the authors put the coverage jump at about 0.08 and the 7-day death rate of uninsured patients just under 65 at 0.05, so the 'any coverage' channel can explain at most about 0.4 points (about 40%) even if coverage prevented every such death; they point to changes in treatment within the same hospitals, not moves to better hospitals.
takeaways:
    - Compare just either side of a rule
    - 'Continuity: nothing else jumps'
    - The answer holds at the line

