    text or_2 "adults under 65" #8f887c x 245 y 340 font_size 36 hidden true
    text takeaway_1 "Compare just either side of a rule" #3d3833 x 300 y 520 font_size 34 hidden true
    text takeaway_2 "Continuity: nothing else jumps" #3d3833 x 300 y 420 font_size 34 hidden true
    text takeaway_3 "The answer holds at the line" #3d3833 x 300 y 320 font_size 34 hidden true
    path big_l #b5482e points [[620,390],[785,440]] style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    path big_r #2f6b8f points [[795,330],[960,380]] style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    path big_c dashed #4f8a4b points [[790,220],[790,560]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path big_s #8a5fa8 points [[795,440],[795,330]] style.stroke_width 9 hidden true
    text big_65 "65" #4f8a4b x 790 y 590 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Who was saved, and how far it reaches"

So who exactly did Medicare save? Most of these patients had insurance before sixty-five, too.
    draw pop p_h

Only about seven in a hundred w (@draw p_new@)ent from no insurance to some at the birthday.
    highlight pop_newly

Yet deaths within a week fell by (@highlight b1_v@) about one point one.
    draw base b1 b1_lab b1_v

And by the authors' own count, about five in a hundred uninsured pati (@highlight pop_newly@)ents just under sixty-five died within a week.
    draw d5

    quiz "If coverage had saved every one of those newly insured patients, how much of the drop of 1.1 points could that explain?" intro "Try the bound yourself."
        right: At most about 0.4 points: five in a hundred of about seven in a hundred, roughly a third of the drop.
        wrong: Take five in a hundred of the seven in a hundred: how many deaths per hundred patients is that?
        * All of it
        + At most about 0.4 points
        * None of it

Seven in a hundred, times five in a hundred, is at most about ze (@draw b2 b2_lab b2_v@)ro point four points, even if coverage saved every one of them.
    draw frm

So most of the gain went to patients who were insured already: something about Medicare changed how they were treated.
    highlight pop_insured

Which part did it, the data cannot say. For the already insured the kind of insurance changes, hospitals are paid differently, and retirement and Social Security cluster near sixty-five.
    highlight b1

Effects split by insurance before sixty-five, and by what hospitals did, would tell those apart. The authors found more care inside the same hospitals, not moves to better ones.
    highlight pop_newly pop_insured

That is also why dividing by the jump in coverage, as a fuzzy design would, misleads here: it assumes Medicare works only through the newly insured.
    highlight p_new

Minus one point one over seven point four points is about fifteen points per newly insured patient, impossible when only five in a hundred die, as the bound just showed.
    highlight b2 b1

    erase pop p_h p_new

Nor does this step speak for people of forty. For them it takes another source, such as Ore (@highlight or_2@)gon's 2008 lottery, which drew names for Medicaid among low-income adults.
    draw or_1 or_2

    erase base b1 b1_lab b1_v b2 b2_lab b2_v frm d5 or_1 or_2

And before trusting any step, researchers stress-test it: other windows, other curve shapes, and checks that nobody sorts to one side of the line.
    draw big_c big_65 big_l big_r big_s

So, can one birthday tell us whether insurance keeps people alive? For very sick patients at sixty-five, it can: about one (@highlight big_s@) death in five within a week was avoided.
    draw takeaway_1

It works because the rule jumps and, as far as anyone can check, n (@highlight big_l big_r@)othing else does. That is continuity, and it stays an assumption.
    draw takeaway_2

And the answer belongs to the line: pe (@highlight big_c@)ople at sixty-five, in California hospitals, in the 1990s.
    draw takeaway_3

A birthday ran the experiment that no one would ever have been allowed to run.
    highlight big_s

## Where next
